# CAP04, the audit devices. A skeleton, and a correction.
#
# THE COMMAND IN MOST TUTORIALS DOES NOT WORK ON 2.6.1.
#
#   bao audit enable file file_path=/openbao/audit/audit.log
#   Code: 400. Errors:
#   * cannot enable audit device via API; use declarative, config-based audit
#     device management instead
#
# Audit devices are server configuration now, which means they go in the Helm
# values for the production cluster and arrive with the process. Better for
# GitOps, and it means enabling auditing on a running cluster is a rolling
# restart rather than an API call. On the CAP01 cluster that restart costs you
# nothing, because every node unseals itself: this is the first time in the
# course that auto-unseal pays for itself in an ordinary operation rather than
# in an incident.
#
# Add these stanzas to the production values' config block, then roll the pods.
# Standbys first, then step down the leader and roll it last.

audit "file" "local-file" {
  options {
    file_path = # ?
  }
}

# THINK. Why a second device at all?
#
# Lesson 5.5 measured the answer: with one device on a full disk, OpenBao
# returns 500 to every request until it can write again. With two, the same full
# disk is an error line in the log and nothing else, because a request is
# audited successfully if ANY device logs it.
#
# The cost is that your audit trail, paths in clear, now also flows wherever this
# device sends it.
audit "file" "stdout" {
  options {
    file_path = # ?
  }
}

# And the volume the file device writes to. In the lab it is a small emptyDir,
# deliberately small, so CAP05's neighbours can fill it. In production it is a
# real disk with alerting on free space, because free space is the only signal
# that arrives before the outage rather than with it.
